<h2>Thursday, July 1,  2004</h2>	
<div class="post">
	 <h3 class="storytitle" id="post-27"><a href="http://www.marksportal.net/index.php?p=27" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Christianity &#038; Marriage">Christianity &#038; Marriage</a></h3>
	<div class="meta">posted by: Mark @ 11:36 pm </div>
	
	<div id="wplinks" class="storycontent">
			<p>Today I saw <em>Spiderman 2</em>.  I enjoyed it much better than the first one.   There&#8217;s a bit of a spoiler here so if  you don&#8217;t want to know about it stop reading. <img src='http://www.marksportal.net/wp/wp-images/smilies/icon_razz.gif' alt=':P' />   As I was driving home today I started thinking about three scenes, all towards the end.   The first was after Spiderman had lowered M.J. down and her fiance came running to comfort her.  As they stood there embrassing each other, M.J. looked up longingly at Parker/Spiderman.  The second scene, was where the Maid of Honor ran up to the fiance at the alter and gave him M.J&#8217;s note.  And the last scene was where M.J.  and Peter stood in the doorway of his apartment kissing.  </p>
	<p>I know this is just a movie, but my thought can&#8217;t help but think about those who are left hurting.  In the first and second scene I mentioned, it&#8217;s the relationship between M.J and her fiance.  Her fiance(I don&#8217;t remember his name) had obviously loved M.J. and trusted in her love for him.  Hence, the marriage.  M.J., though she accepted the proposal, obviously did not feel the same about him.   As the third scene started to play I was wondering if they were going to show the girl who brought Peter the cake.  I half expected to see her in the background watching as the two kissed, with a feeling of hurt.   </p>
	<p>I don&#8217;t know why, but for some reason it&#8217;s these situations that stand out in my mind.  How can anyone truly trust in another person&#8217;s love?   You really can&#8217;t.  You can make a best effort guess, but over time people change, personalities change, and even feelings change.  I guess at some point you just like a person enough that your willing to chance the hurt and pain of being left, for the hope of a person&#8217;s love.  That is just what these characters did.  They took that risk&#8230;and lost.  And though the risks I have taken in my life have been small and few, I find that it is with these characters that I identify.  I become the person left at the alter.  I become the person left watching in the doorway.  When the movie screen fades to black and the lights come back on, there I sit having the full range of emotions of having seen things through that character&#8217;s eyes.  Feeling as that character felt.  I walk away emotionally drained.</p>
	<p>For the last two weeks I&#8217;ve been thinking a lot about the Christian life and marriage.  These scripture verses  keep tugging at my brain,</p>
	<p><em>&#8220;Jesus said to them, “The sons of this age marry and are given in marriage, but those who are considered worthy to attain to that age and the resurrection from the dead, neither marry nor are given in marriage; &#8221; (Luke 20:34-35, NASB95) </em></p>
	<p><em>&#8220;But I want you to be free from concern. One who is unmarried is concerned about the things of the Lord, how he may please the Lord; but one who is married is concerned about the things of the world, how he may please his wife, and his interests are divided. &#8221; (1 Corinthians 7:32-34, NASB95) </em></p>
	<p><em>&#8220;Or do you not know, brethren (for I am speaking to those who know the law), that the law has jurisdiction over a person as long as he lives? For the married woman is bound by law to her husband while he is living; but if her husband dies, she is released from the law concerning the husband. So then, if while her husband is living she is joined to another man, she shall be called an adulteress; but if her husband dies, she is free from the law, so that she is not an adulteress though she is joined to another man. &#8221; (Romans 7:1-3, NASB95) </em></p>
	<p><em>&#8220;Are you bound to a wife? Do not seek to be released. Are you released from a wife? Do not seek a wife. But if you marry, you have not sinned; and if a virgin marries, she has not sinned. Yet such will have trouble in this life, and I am trying to spare you. But this I say, brethren, the time has been shortened, so that from now on those who have wives should be as though they had none; and those who weep, as though they did not weep; and those who rejoice, as though they did not rejoice; and those who buy, as though they did not possess; and those who use the world, as though they did not make full use of it; for the form of this world is passing away. &#8221; (1 Corinthians 7:27-31, NASB95) </em></p>
	<p><em>&#8220;He who finds a wife finds a good thing And obtains favor from the Lord. &#8221; (Proverbs 18:22, NASB95)</em></p>
	<p>Phew, lots of stuff in there.  So based on these scripture verses we gather these things:  The marriage contract is meant for life on earth and is only valid until death&#8230;.it does not extend to heaven.  There will be no marriages in heaven.  Marriage is a blessing from God and should not be looked at as bad.  However, if you are single, that too should be counted as a blessing, for you are able to devote your whole attention to serving the Lord.</p>
	<p>In a previous posting I talked about spiritual gifts and how they were meant for our time here on earth&#8230;..that they will not necessarily be extended to heaven.   Marriage seems to be the same way.  It is meant for this time, however when we die and God creates a new heaven and new earth, these things will no longer be around.  I find it interesting and annoying the amount of focus we put on finding that &#8220;right person".   As I mentioned a few paragraphs earlier, we take great risks and strides in hope of finding someone who we can love and in return love us.   Yet it will all fade away.   And though I have thought and written about all of this, I still find that my desire for finding that mate is still there.   A constant struggle between my emotions/desire and my mind.
</p>
